          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022pages/concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining and ranking results from multiple retrieval methods, with emphasis on hybrid retrieval.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-23T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/RAG\\n  - pattern/HybridRetrieval\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022One substantive source on hybrid retrieval mechanisms and production adoption; reciprocal rank fusion usage widely known but not yet independently corroborated in this wiki.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval is the dominant pattern in modern retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) systems. It combines two complementary search strategies:\\n\\n1. **Sparse lexical search** (e.g. BM25) \\u2014 excels at exact term matches and rare identifiers\\n2. **Dense vector search** \\u2014 captures semantic similarity and conceptual relationships\\n\\nThese two ranked result sets are then merged, typically via **reciprocal rank fusion**, to produce a single ranked output that leverages the strengths of both approaches.\\n\\n### Why Hybrid Works\\n\\nHybrid retrieval consistently outperforms either method alone, particularly on queries that mix:\\n- **Rare identifiers** (error codes, product SKUs, model names) \\u2014 where lexical precision dominates\\n- **Natural language intent** \\u2014 where semantic similarity matters\\n\\nThis versatility has made hybrid retrieval the **default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems**.\\n\\n### Reciprocal Rank Fusion\\n\\nReciprocal rank fusion is a simple but effective merging strategy: it normalises ranks from both result sets and combines them, allowing both high-ranking lexical matches and high-ranking semantic matches to surface in the final result.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Hybrid retrieval design and production adoption (eval://update-over-duplicate/sample-07)\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
      "assistant_text": "Good, the log doesn\u0027t exist yet. Now I understand the wiki state:\n\n**Current State:**\n- One concept page on \u0060retrieval-patterns\u0060 with minimal content (mentions reciprocal rank fusion but lacks depth)\n- The existing page description matches the scope of hybrid retrieval, which is a specific retrieval pattern\n- This is an ideal case for updating, not creating a new page\n\n**Editorial Decision:**\nThe source provides concrete, detailed information about hybrid retrieval\u2014its methodology (combining BM25 with dense vectors via reciprocal rank fusion), its use cases (handling rare identifiers \u002B natural language intent), and its adoption status (production RAG default). This directly enhances the existing retrieval-patterns page.\n\nI\u0027ll update the page to incorporate this information, expand it properly, and improve the frontmatter with better tags and confidence scoring.",
